1
01:00:00,033 --> 01:00:04,233
<b>On August 8th 1947, a draft map was sent</b>

2
01:00:04,233 --> 01:00:06,699
<b>from the Viceroy's office in New Delhi to</b>

3
01:00:06,699 --> 01:00:07,833
<b>the governor of Punjab.</b>

4
01:00:08,833 --> 01:00:10,033
<b>It showed the new border</b>

5
01:00:10,033 --> 01:00:11,566
<b>between India and Pakistan.</b>

6
01:00:12,033 --> 01:00:14,333
<b>Ferozepur, a district that was 55%</b>

7
01:00:14,733 --> 01:00:17,133
<b>Muslim, containing the canal headworks</b>

8
01:00:17,133 --> 01:00:19,066
<b>that controlled irrigation for millions</b>

9
01:00:19,066 --> 01:00:21,000
<b>of acres of Pakistani farmland,</b>

10
01:00:21,766 --> 01:00:23,133
<b>was on the Pakistani side.</b>

11
01:00:23,933 --> 01:00:25,366
<b>Three days later, a</b>

12
01:00:25,366 --> 01:00:27,066
<b>single coded message arrived.</b>

13
01:00:28,033 --> 01:00:28,633
<b>Two words.</b>

14
01:00:29,566 --> 01:00:31,133
<b>Eliminate salient.</b>

15
01:00:31,866 --> 01:00:33,633
<b>Ferozepur was now Indian.</b>

16
01:00:34,433 --> 01:00:36,599
<b>And with it, the valve controlling</b>

17
01:00:36,599 --> 01:00:38,133
<b>Pakistan's water supply.</b>

18
01:00:39,033 --> 01:00:40,599
<b>No census data changed.</b>

19
01:00:41,133 --> 01:00:42,599
<b>No infrastructure moved.</b>

20
01:00:43,266 --> 01:00:45,033
<b>No new evidence was submitted.</b>

21
01:00:46,000 --> 01:00:47,400
<b>The man who drew the border,</b>

22
01:00:48,199 --> 01:00:50,433
<b>a British chancery lawyer who had never</b>

23
01:00:50,433 --> 01:00:51,566
<b>been east of Gibraltar,</b>

24
01:00:52,433 --> 01:00:54,500
<b>who was simultaneously drawing a second</b>

25
01:00:54,500 --> 01:00:57,333
<b>border for Bengal from a desk in Simla,</b>

26
01:00:57,833 --> 01:00:59,300
<b>using maps he later</b>

27
01:00:59,300 --> 01:01:00,633
<b>admitted were incorrect,</b>

28
01:01:01,433 --> 01:01:02,466
<b>burned every working</b>

29
01:01:02,466 --> 01:01:04,333
<b>paper and left India forever.</b>

30
01:01:05,733 --> 01:01:07,933
<b>So, I read the actual documents.</b>

31
01:01:08,400 --> 01:01:09,333
<b>The Radcliffe Award.</b>

32
01:01:09,933 --> 01:01:11,733
<b>The Boundary Commission proceedings.</b>

33
01:01:12,500 --> 01:01:14,066
<b>The transfer of power files.</b>

34
01:01:14,699 --> 01:01:16,833
<b>And what they show is a three-day window</b>

35
01:01:16,833 --> 01:01:19,099
<b>where the most consequential border in</b>

36
01:01:19,099 --> 01:01:21,366
<b>modern South Asian history was redrawn.</b>

37
01:01:22,000 --> 01:01:23,433
<b>And nobody can prove why.</b>

38
01:01:24,199 --> 01:01:25,933
<b>Here's who they choose to draw the line.</b>

39
01:01:26,900 --> 01:01:27,933
<b>Cyril Radcliffe.</b>

40
01:01:28,566 --> 01:01:29,966
<b>48 years old.</b>

41
01:01:30,300 --> 01:01:31,333
<b>A chancery lawyer.</b>

42
01:01:31,733 --> 01:01:32,966
<b>His career was trusts,</b>

43
01:01:33,400 --> 01:01:35,166
<b>estates, corporate structures.</b>

44
01:01:35,966 --> 01:01:38,133
<b>His wartime job was Director General of</b>

45
01:01:38,133 --> 01:01:39,366
<b>the Ministry of Information.</b>

46
01:01:40,066 --> 01:01:42,266
<b>He'd never adjudicated a border dispute.</b>

47
01:01:42,966 --> 01:01:44,266
<b>He'd never studied demographics.</b>

48
01:01:45,199 --> 01:01:47,000
<b>He'd never even set foot in Asia.</b>

49
01:01:47,500 --> 01:01:48,333
<b>According to Stanley</b>

50
01:01:48,333 --> 01:01:50,633
<b>Wolpert in Jinnah of Pakistan,</b>

51
01:01:51,533 --> 01:01:53,533
<b>Radcliffe, who had never even visited</b>

52
01:01:53,533 --> 01:01:55,199
<b>India and expressed no</b>

53
01:01:55,199 --> 01:01:56,900
<b>known opinions on its problems,</b>

54
01:01:57,500 --> 01:01:59,766
<b>was unanimously accepted and would soon</b>

55
01:01:59,766 --> 01:02:01,366
<b>decide the destiny of millions</b>

56
01:02:01,366 --> 01:02:03,900
<b>of Hindus, Sikhs and Muslims.</b>

57
01:02:04,933 --> 01:02:06,033
<b>That was the qualification.</b>

58
01:02:07,233 --> 01:02:08,033
<b>Total ignorance.</b>

59
01:02:09,066 --> 01:02:09,933
<b>A man with no</b>

60
01:02:09,933 --> 01:02:13,166
<b>attachments, no opinions, no stake.</b>

61
01:02:13,833 --> 01:02:14,866
<b>Supposedly impartial.</b>

62
01:02:15,766 --> 01:02:17,533
<b>Precisely because he knew nothing.</b>

63
01:02:18,666 --> 01:02:20,566
<b>He arrived on July 8th.</b>

64
01:02:20,666 --> 01:02:22,133
<b>He had until mid-August.</b>

65
01:02:23,133 --> 01:02:25,000
<b>36 days to draw a border</b>

66
01:02:25,000 --> 01:02:26,800
<b>for 400 million people.</b>

67
01:02:27,833 --> 01:02:28,533
<b>And here's the part</b>

68
01:02:28,533 --> 01:02:29,800
<b>that rarely gets mentioned.</b>

69
01:02:30,166 --> 01:02:32,300
<b>He wasn't drawing one border.</b>

70
01:02:32,966 --> 01:02:34,666
<b>He was chairing two boundary</b>

71
01:02:34,666 --> 01:02:36,266
<b>commissions simultaneously.</b>

72
01:02:37,400 --> 01:02:38,400
<b>Punjab and Bengal.</b>

73
01:02:39,133 --> 01:02:41,033
<b>He couldn't physically attend the Punjab</b>

74
01:02:41,033 --> 01:02:43,300
<b>public hearings in Lahore because he was</b>

75
01:02:43,300 --> 01:02:45,733
<b>processing Bengal paperwork from Delhi.</b>

76
01:02:46,766 --> 01:02:48,133
<b>As Yasmin Khan writes,</b>

77
01:02:48,566 --> 01:02:51,433
<b>"The border was devised from a distance.</b>

78
01:02:51,933 --> 01:02:54,233
<b>The land, villages and communities to be</b>

79
01:02:54,233 --> 01:02:57,166
<b>divided were not visited or inspected by</b>

80
01:02:57,166 --> 01:02:58,633
<b>the imperial map maker."</b>

81
01:02:59,433 --> 01:03:00,466
<b>He later told the</b>

82
01:03:00,466 --> 01:03:01,866
<b>journalist Kuldip Nayar</b>

83
01:03:02,633 --> 01:03:04,833
<b>"Even district maps were not available</b>

84
01:03:04,833 --> 01:03:07,133
<b>and the ones there were were incorrect."</b>

85
01:03:07,533 --> 01:03:09,900
<b>His official instructions, the terms of</b>

86
01:03:09,900 --> 01:03:11,766
<b>reference, said this.</b>

87
01:03:11,766 --> 01:03:13,933
<b>"The boundary commission is instructed to</b>

88
01:03:13,933 --> 01:03:15,833
<b>demarcate the boundaries on the basis of</b>

89
01:03:15,900 --> 01:03:17,766
<b>ascertaining the contiguous majority</b>

90
01:03:17,766 --> 01:03:20,066
<b>areas of Muslims and non-Muslims.</b>

91
01:03:20,866 --> 01:03:23,066
<b>In doing so, it will also take into</b>

92
01:03:23,066 --> 01:03:25,099
<b>account other factors."</b>

93
01:03:26,000 --> 01:03:29,166
<b>Other factors never defined. It could</b>

94
01:03:29,166 --> 01:03:31,966
<b>mean railways, canals, economic</b>

95
01:03:31,966 --> 01:03:33,800
<b>viability, anything.</b>

96
01:03:34,633 --> 01:03:35,833
<b>So the mandate was</b>

97
01:03:35,833 --> 01:03:37,333
<b>follow the demographics.</b>

98
01:03:38,033 --> 01:03:40,033
<b>Unless you decide not to.</b>

99
01:03:40,500 --> 01:03:41,599
<b>And the demographic data</b>

100
01:03:41,599 --> 01:03:43,266
<b>was from a 1941 census.</b>

101
01:03:44,000 --> 01:03:46,766
<b>Six years out of date Radcliffe chaired a</b>

102
01:03:46,766 --> 01:03:49,133
<b>four-member commission. Two judges</b>

103
01:03:49,133 --> 01:03:50,633
<b>nominated by the Muslim League.</b>

104
01:03:51,033 --> 01:03:52,933
<b>Two by congress and the Sikhs.</b>

105
01:03:53,433 --> 01:03:54,866
<b>They deadlocked on every</b>

106
01:03:54,866 --> 01:03:57,066
<b>issue. Two against two.</b>

107
01:03:57,866 --> 01:03:58,466
<b>So the Indian</b>

108
01:03:58,466 --> 01:04:01,133
<b>Independence Act was amended so that</b>

109
01:04:01,366 --> 01:04:02,733
<b>award meant the</b>

110
01:04:02,733 --> 01:04:04,166
<b>decision of the chairman alone.</b>

111
01:04:04,933 --> 01:04:06,533
<b>One man making all the calls.</b>

112
01:04:07,500 --> 01:04:09,666
<b>Patrick French in Liberty or Death</b>

113
01:04:10,133 --> 01:04:12,233
<b>documents what happened on August 8th.</b>

114
01:04:12,733 --> 01:04:14,166
<b>On 8th August,</b>

115
01:04:14,666 --> 01:04:16,933
<b>George Abell sent a rough map featuring</b>

116
01:04:16,933 --> 01:04:19,066
<b>the Ferozepur salient to the private</b>

117
01:04:19,066 --> 01:04:20,933
<b>office officer, Evan Jenkins.</b>

118
01:04:21,833 --> 01:04:24,233
<b>An accompanying letter by Abell said</b>

119
01:04:24,233 --> 01:04:25,800
<b>"there will not be any great</b>

120
01:04:25,800 --> 01:04:27,400
<b>changes from this boundary".</b>

121
01:04:27,933 --> 01:04:28,766
<b>Abell was Lord</b>

122
01:04:28,766 --> 01:04:30,666
<b>Mountbatten's private secretary.</b>

123
01:04:31,433 --> 01:04:33,033
<b>Jenkins was the governor of Punjab.</b>

124
01:04:33,966 --> 01:04:36,033
<b>He'd been begging for advance information</b>

125
01:04:36,033 --> 01:04:38,233
<b>so he could deploy police before the</b>

126
01:04:38,233 --> 01:04:39,266
<b>border was announced.</b>

127
01:04:39,666 --> 01:04:40,933
<b>But here's why the August</b>

128
01:04:40,933 --> 01:04:43,433
<b>8th map made geographic sense.</b>

129
01:04:44,000 --> 01:04:47,166
<b>Ferozepur Tehsil was 55% Muslim.</b>

130
01:04:47,666 --> 01:04:50,566
<b>Zira Tehsil was 65% Muslim. The</b>

131
01:04:50,566 --> 01:04:51,533
<b>demographic mandate</b>

132
01:04:51,533 --> 01:04:53,366
<b>pointed clearly to Pakistan.</b>

133
01:04:54,300 --> 01:04:56,433
<b>But these Tehsils weren't just farmland.</b>

134
01:04:57,033 --> 01:04:58,866
<b>They contained the headworks of the Sutlej</b>

135
01:04:58,866 --> 01:05:00,666
<b>Valley canal system.</b>

136
01:05:01,066 --> 01:05:02,833
<b>The physical infrastructure that</b>

137
01:05:02,833 --> 01:05:04,633
<b>controlled water flow to millions of</b>

138
01:05:04,633 --> 01:05:06,633
<b>acres of irrigated land downstream.</b>

139
01:05:07,433 --> 01:05:09,199
<b>Land that after</b>

140
01:05:09,199 --> 01:05:11,166
<b>partition would be in Pakistan.</b>

141
01:05:12,266 --> 01:05:13,366
<b>Think of it as a valve.</b>

142
01:05:14,166 --> 01:05:16,800
<b>Whoever held Ferozepur held a tap.</b>

143
01:05:17,866 --> 01:05:20,566
<b>And the August 8th draft gave the tap to</b>

144
01:05:20,566 --> 01:05:22,300
<b>the same country that needed the water.</b>

145
01:05:23,300 --> 01:05:24,900
<b>The letter said "there will</b>

146
01:05:24,900 --> 01:05:26,233
<b>not be any great changes".</b>

147
01:05:27,266 --> 01:05:29,233
<b>Then something changed.</b>

148
01:05:29,833 --> 01:05:32,266
<b>Two or three days later Jenkins received</b>

149
01:05:32,266 --> 01:05:33,833
<b>a coded telephone message. A</b>

150
01:05:34,000 --> 01:05:35,466
<b>Secraphone call from the</b>

151
01:05:35,466 --> 01:05:37,433
<b>Viceroy's house. French again.</b>

152
01:05:38,133 --> 01:05:40,133
<b>Jenkins received a Secraphone message</b>

153
01:05:40,133 --> 01:05:42,766
<b>telling him to eliminate salient.</b>

154
01:05:43,599 --> 01:05:45,166
<b>Meaning that the whole of Ferozepur</b>

155
01:05:45,166 --> 01:05:47,400
<b>district would now remain in India.</b>

156
01:05:48,133 --> 01:05:50,733
<b>48 000 Muslim residents according to the</b>

157
01:05:50,733 --> 01:05:53,966
<b>1941 census. This now became Indian.</b>

158
01:05:54,533 --> 01:05:55,733
<b>Erased from Pakistan's</b>

159
01:05:55,733 --> 01:05:57,366
<b>side with a single phone call.</b>

160
01:05:57,933 --> 01:06:00,400
<b>And with them the canal headworks.</b>

161
01:06:01,033 --> 01:06:01,933
<b>The valve shut.</b>

162
01:06:02,666 --> 01:06:04,966
<b>Within months India would unilaterally</b>

163
01:06:04,966 --> 01:06:07,466
<b>cut off water to Pakistani canals.</b>

164
01:06:07,966 --> 01:06:09,833
<b>Triggering an irrigation crisis that took</b>

165
01:06:09,833 --> 01:06:11,266
<b>12 years to resolve.</b>

166
01:06:12,533 --> 01:06:14,000
<b>So what happened between</b>

167
01:06:14,000 --> 01:06:16,300
<b>August 8th and August 10th?</b>

168
01:06:16,300 --> 01:06:19,366
<b>On August 9th Radcliffe met Lord Ismay.</b>

169
01:06:19,866 --> 01:06:22,400
<b>Mountbatten's chief of staff for an evening</b>

170
01:06:22,400 --> 01:06:24,133
<b>drink at the Viceroy's estate.</b>

171
01:06:24,666 --> 01:06:25,433
<b>The meeting is</b>

172
01:06:25,433 --> 01:06:27,733
<b>documented but as Khan writes</b>

173
01:06:28,166 --> 01:06:30,266
<b>"there is no contemporary record of it</b>

174
01:06:30,266 --> 01:06:32,466
<b>and the recollections of the three</b>

175
01:06:32,466 --> 01:06:33,566
<b>participants differ".</b>

176
01:06:34,566 --> 01:06:36,833
<b>Years later Christopher Beaumont,</b>

177
01:06:37,500 --> 01:06:38,533
<b>Radcliffe's own private</b>

178
01:06:38,533 --> 01:06:40,533
<b>secretary gave testimony.</b>

179
01:06:41,166 --> 01:06:45,500
<b>In 1989 and again in 1992 Beaumont stated</b>

180
01:06:45,500 --> 01:06:47,800
<b>that Mountbatten allegedly</b>

181
01:06:47,800 --> 01:06:49,433
<b>under pressure from Nehru</b>

182
01:06:49,833 --> 01:06:51,566
<b>persuaded Radcliffe to change the</b>

183
01:06:51,566 --> 01:06:53,566
<b>Punjab's border line in India's favor.</b>

184
01:06:54,566 --> 01:06:57,066
<b>And there's more. John Christie,</b>

185
01:06:57,666 --> 01:06:59,033
<b>Mountbatten's joint private</b>

186
01:06:59,033 --> 01:07:00,766
<b>secretary, kept a diary.</b>

187
01:07:01,633 --> 01:07:04,766
<b>His entry for August 9th says Mountbatten</b>

188
01:07:04,766 --> 01:07:07,566
<b>was in a tired flap and had to be</b>

189
01:07:07,599 --> 01:07:09,666
<b>strenuously dissuaded from asking</b>

190
01:07:09,666 --> 01:07:11,366
<b>Radcliffe to alter his award.</b>

191
01:07:12,033 --> 01:07:15,033
<b>Strenuously dissuaded. Meaning someone</b>

192
01:07:15,033 --> 01:07:16,633
<b>had to talk him out of it.</b>

193
01:07:16,699 --> 01:07:19,166
<b>Which suggests he was trying to do it.</b>

194
01:07:19,933 --> 01:07:22,433
<b>If we're being fair, the evidence against</b>

195
01:07:22,433 --> 01:07:23,566
<b>intervention is real.</b>

196
01:07:24,333 --> 01:07:25,166
<b>Mountbatten maintained</b>

197
01:07:25,166 --> 01:07:27,033
<b>that he and Radcliffe were</b>

198
01:07:27,266 --> 01:07:30,066
<b>"mutually most careful never to have any</b>

199
01:07:30,066 --> 01:07:31,533
<b>discussions about the boundaries".</b>

200
01:07:32,400 --> 01:07:34,766
<b>Radcliffe himself called his award the</b>

201
01:07:34,766 --> 01:07:37,033
<b>result of his own unfettered judgment.</b>

202
01:07:37,866 --> 01:07:40,433
<b>And the "other factors clause" does</b>

203
01:07:40,433 --> 01:07:41,933
<b>provide a legitimate rationale.</b>

204
01:07:42,866 --> 01:07:44,666
<b>Radcliffe wrote "I have</b>

205
01:07:44,666 --> 01:07:46,533
<b>hesitated long over those not</b>

206
01:07:46,666 --> 01:07:48,066
<b>in considerable areas</b>

207
01:07:48,066 --> 01:07:49,733
<b>east of the Sutlej River.</b>

208
01:07:50,233 --> 01:07:52,066
<b>I have come to the conclusion that there</b>

209
01:07:52,066 --> 01:07:53,333
<b>are factors such as</b>

210
01:07:53,333 --> 01:07:54,433
<b>the disruption of railway</b>

211
01:07:54,466 --> 01:07:57,333
<b>communications and water systems that</b>

212
01:07:57,333 --> 01:07:59,066
<b>ought in this instance to</b>

213
01:07:59,066 --> 01:08:00,766
<b>displace the primary claims of</b>

214
01:08:00,766 --> 01:08:03,666
<b>contiguous majorities. Canal headworks,</b>

215
01:08:04,266 --> 01:08:06,366
<b>railway networks, these are real</b>

216
01:08:06,366 --> 01:08:07,633
<b>infrastructure concerns.</b>

217
01:08:08,433 --> 01:08:11,366
<b>So you could read these two ways. Either</b>

218
01:08:11,366 --> 01:08:12,400
<b>Mountbatten pressured</b>

219
01:08:12,400 --> 01:08:13,666
<b>Radcliffe to change the line</b>

220
01:08:14,133 --> 01:08:16,366
<b>and Radcliffe retrospectively justified</b>

221
01:08:16,366 --> 01:08:18,399
<b>it. Or Radcliffe</b>

222
01:08:18,399 --> 01:08:20,233
<b>genuinely reconsidered his draft</b>

223
01:08:20,233 --> 01:08:21,233
<b>and reached a different</b>

224
01:08:21,233 --> 01:08:23,066
<b>conclusion on legitimate grounds.</b>

225
01:08:23,433 --> 01:08:24,666
<b>We won't know for sure</b>

226
01:08:24,666 --> 01:08:26,333
<b>because of what happened next.</b>

227
01:08:26,833 --> 01:08:28,533
<b>Radcliffe signed the complete award on</b>

228
01:08:28,533 --> 01:08:29,899
<b>August 12th. It was</b>

229
01:08:29,899 --> 01:08:31,899
<b>published on August 17th.</b>

230
01:08:31,899 --> 01:08:33,966
<b>Two days after both India and Pakistan</b>

231
01:08:33,966 --> 01:08:35,399
<b>celebrated independence.</b>

232
01:08:36,366 --> 01:08:38,600
<b>People in borderland villages didn't know</b>

233
01:08:38,600 --> 01:08:40,033
<b>which country they lived in</b>

234
01:08:40,033 --> 01:08:41,733
<b>until two days after their</b>

235
01:08:41,733 --> 01:08:43,033
<b>countries already existed.</b>

236
01:08:43,833 --> 01:08:46,666
<b>And then Radcliffe destroyed everything.</b>

237
01:08:47,266 --> 01:08:48,966
<b>Aloys Arthur Michel confirms.</b>

238
01:08:49,733 --> 01:08:52,533
<b>Sir Cyril's personal notes, memoranda and</b>

239
01:08:52,533 --> 01:08:54,733
<b>drafts were all destroyed at that time.</b>

240
01:08:55,533 --> 01:08:56,766
<b>Every working paper,</b>

241
01:08:57,233 --> 01:08:58,633
<b>every draft map gone.</b>

242
01:08:59,233 --> 01:09:02,066
<b>He refused his salary. He never returned</b>

243
01:09:02,066 --> 01:09:03,066
<b>to the subcontinent.</b>

244
01:09:03,733 --> 01:09:06,766
<b>Months before his death in 1977 someone</b>

245
01:09:06,766 --> 01:09:08,633
<b>asked if he'd ever go back.</b>

246
01:09:09,366 --> 01:09:10,233
<b>His answer?</b>

247
01:09:11,433 --> 01:09:13,733
<b>God forbid. Not even if they asked me.</b>

248
01:09:14,166 --> 01:09:15,333
<b>I suspect they'd shoot me</b>

249
01:09:15,333 --> 01:09:16,966
<b>out of hand. Both sides.</b>

250
01:09:18,600 --> 01:09:20,466
<b>Ferozepur gets the most attention.</b>

251
01:09:20,933 --> 01:09:21,466
<b>But the bigger</b>

252
01:09:21,466 --> 01:09:23,266
<b>consequence may be Gurdaspur.</b>

253
01:09:23,833 --> 01:09:26,733
<b>Gurdaspur contains the Pathankot Road, the</b>

254
01:09:26,733 --> 01:09:29,233
<b>only land corridor from India to Kashmir.</b>

255
01:09:29,833 --> 01:09:32,466
<b>Michel is direct. Had Radcliffe awarded</b>

256
01:09:32,466 --> 01:09:33,733
<b>Gurdaspur to Pakistan,</b>

257
01:09:34,133 --> 01:09:35,366
<b>there would have been no land</b>

258
01:09:35,366 --> 01:09:36,433
<b>communication between</b>

259
01:09:36,433 --> 01:09:38,266
<b>India and Jammu Kashmir.</b>

260
01:09:38,666 --> 01:09:41,466
<b>In 1965, 18 years after</b>

261
01:09:41,466 --> 01:09:43,733
<b>drawing the line, Radcliffe said,</b>

262
01:09:44,333 --> 01:09:46,266
<b>"I wouldn't dream of defending some of</b>

263
01:09:46,266 --> 01:09:47,066
<b>the decisions in</b>

264
01:09:47,066 --> 01:09:48,666
<b>detail I made at the time.</b>

265
01:09:49,133 --> 01:09:50,266
<b>I was not aware of the</b>

266
01:09:50,266 --> 01:09:51,333
<b>Kashmir thing at all."</b>

267
01:09:51,633 --> 01:09:52,466
<b>He says he wasn't</b>

268
01:09:52,466 --> 01:09:53,866
<b>even thinking of Kashmir.</b>

269
01:09:54,533 --> 01:09:56,166
<b>If that's true, the road</b>

270
01:09:56,166 --> 01:09:57,399
<b>corridor was an accident.</b>

271
01:09:57,866 --> 01:09:59,699
<b>If it's not, it was the most</b>

272
01:09:59,699 --> 01:10:01,600
<b>consequential geographic decision in</b>

273
01:10:01,600 --> 01:10:03,133
<b>modern South Asian history.</b>

274
01:10:03,500 --> 01:10:05,399
<b>The scholarly consensus is that this</b>

275
01:10:05,399 --> 01:10:06,633
<b>wasn't a grand conspiracy.</b>

276
01:10:07,233 --> 01:10:10,133
<b>It was a shoddy implementation of a</b>

277
01:10:10,133 --> 01:10:12,600
<b>wafer-thin plan, exhaustion,</b>

278
01:10:13,100 --> 01:10:15,566
<b>haste and a desire to escape liability.</b>

279
01:10:16,766 --> 01:10:18,100
<b>But here's what bothers me.</b>

280
01:10:18,500 --> 01:10:19,933
<b>We'll never know for certain what</b>

281
01:10:19,933 --> 01:10:22,199
<b>happened on August 9th, 1947.</b>

282
01:10:23,199 --> 01:10:24,533
<b>Because the only man who could have</b>

283
01:10:24,533 --> 01:10:27,300
<b>settled it burned the evidence and took</b>

284
01:10:27,300 --> 01:10:28,500
<b>the answer to his grave.</b>

285
01:10:29,833 --> 01:10:30,766
<b>Those are the documents.</b>

286
01:10:31,433 --> 01:10:33,866
<b>What you conclude from them is up to you.</b>

287
01:10:34,166 --> 01:10:35,566
<b>Full sources in the description.</b>

288
01:10:36,366 --> 01:10:37,100
<b>Thank you for watching.</b>

